/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode() : val(0), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x, TreeNode *left, TreeNode *right)
 *     : val(x), left(left), right(right) {}
 * };
 */

/*
Problem: Construct Binary Search Tree from Preorder Traversal (LC 1008)
Topic: Binary Search Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

In preorder traversal:

Root → Left → Right

The first element is always the root.

Instead of searching for the first
greater element to separate the left
and right subtrees, maintain an
upper bound.

If the current preorder element is
greater than the bound, it does not
belong to the current subtree.

Recursively construct:

• Left subtree with bound = root->val

• Right subtree with the parent's
  bound

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Maintain an index 'i' pointing to
   the current preorder element.

2. If:

   • i reaches the end, or

   • preorder[i] > bound,

   return NULL.

3. Create a node using preorder[i].

4. Increment i.

5. Build the left subtree using
   bound = root->val.

6. Build the right subtree using
   the parent's bound.

7. Return the root.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

preorder = [8,5,1,7,10,12]

Constructed BST:

        8
       / \
      5   10
     / \    \
    1   7    12

--------------------------------------------------

Dry Run:

preorder = [8,5,1,7,10,12]

helper(bound = ∞)

Create 8

Left subtree:

helper(bound = 8)

Create 5

Left subtree:

helper(bound = 5)

Create 1

Next value = 7

7 > 5

Return NULL

Right subtree:

7 > 5

Return NULL

Return node 1

Back to node 5

Right subtree:

helper(bound = 8)

Create 7

Next value = 10

10 > 7

Return NULL

Return node 7

Back to node 8

Right subtree:

helper(bound = ∞)

Create 10

Left subtree:

12 > 10

Return NULL

Right subtree:

Create 12

Done.

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The preorder array is traversed only
once.

The 'bound' ensures that every value
is placed in the correct subtree.

When a value exceeds the current
bound, recursion returns to the
parent, allowing that value to be
placed in the appropriate subtree.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Each preorder element is processed
exactly once.

--------------------------------------------------

Space : O(h)

Recursion stack.

Balanced BST:

O(log n)

Worst case:

O(n)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Binary Search Tree
Recursion
Preorder Traversal
*/

class Solution {
public:

    TreeNode* helper(vector<int>& preorder,
                     int& i,
                     int bound) {

        // Base case
        if (i >= preorder.size() ||
            preorder[i] > bound)
            return NULL;

        // Create current root
        TreeNode* root =
            new TreeNode(preorder[i++]);

        // Build left subtree
        root->left =
            helper(preorder, i, root->val);

        // Build right subtree
        root->right =
            helper(preorder, i, bound);

        return root;
    }

    TreeNode* bstFromPreorder(vector<int>& preorder) {

        int i = 0;

        return helper(preorder, i, INT_MAX);
    }
};
